# Coroutines

- a procedure that collaborates with a caller, yielding and receiving data from the caller
(can be used to implement cooperative multitasking)
- syntax -- contains yield keyword on a right side of expression (yield -- to produce and to give way) e.g. datum = yield, evolved from generators...
- coroutuine receives data from the caller through .send(data)
- coroutine can also use *return*
- coroutine can use *yield from* to delegate to a subgenerator
- coro can be in 4 states, use inspect.getgeneratorstate() to find out:
  gen_created, gen_running, gen_suspended, gen_closed

- the first call next(coro) is called "priming" (advancing it to the first yield to make it ready for use as a live coroutine)
- coroutine is suspended exactly at the yield keyword, assignement happens on the send() call with the pushed value

def simple_coro(a):
  print(a)
  b = yield a
  print(b)
  c = yield a + b
  print c

my_coro = simple_coro(1)
next(my_coro) #priming
-> 1
my_coro.send(2)
-> 2
my_coro.send(3)
-> 5
then StopIteration is returned, no reset button exists, one needs to instantiate coroutine again

Unhandled exceptions propagate from the coroutine to the caller, generator.throw() and generator.close() can be used to send exceptions to coroutines

# Usint yield from (replaced by await keyword)
- allows for generator nesting
- creates a bidirectional channel between caller, delegating generator and subgenerator
